combination. Similarly \text{{\igfont 𐑦}} is $\mathbf{b}$: the system is simultaneously finite (its boundary is finite)
and infinite (its bulk is fully reconstructible from that boundary) --- the imscriptive property is precisely the
co-presence of two contradictory scale statements.
N\={a}g\={a}rjuna's structuralism --- that entities lack inherent existence and exist only relationally --- maps to the
grammar's pre-grammatical basis: objects are positions in categorical structure (\pilcrow{}I), and the grammar's
primitives are relational invariants, not intrinsic properties. This is not coincidence. Both proceed from the same
starting observation: the identity of a thing is determined by its morphisms, not its substrate.

